22:04:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Fair pushback, but that's overstating it — let me clarify what I actually meant. ## The "ouch" is brief; the aftermath is where painkillers shine That very first sharp jolt from stubbing your toe or slicing your finger lasts maybe a second or two—it's fast nerve fibers (A-delta) firing off a "damage!" alarm. That's basically un-blockable by NSAIDs/acetaminophen, and honestly, it's such a short blip that it barely matters in the grand scheme. What comes *after* that first jolt is usually the bulk of your actual suffering: - The throbbing ache - The swelling and tenderness - Pain that gets worse when you touch or move the area - Discomfort that lingers for hours or days **This later phase is driven by inflammation and prostaglandins**—exactly what NSAIDs target. So if you already have ibuprofen in your system when you get hurt, it *will* blunt the inflammatory buildup and reduce how bad things feel over the next several hours, even though it didn't stop that initial flinch-worthy spike. ## So are they "doing nothing" for new injuries? No—they're doing a lot, just not instantaneously. Think of it less like "an invisible force field against all pain" and more like "actively working to keep the fire from getting worse" once tissue damage triggers inflammation. You just won't notice that effect in the first second, because that first second isn't really what they're designed for. ## Why it might *feel* like they did nothing If you already took a painkiller, then get hurt, and it still hurts a lot—you may be comparing the initial spike (which no OTC drug touches anyway) to your memory of times you took a painkiller *after* the swelling/inflammation had already built up and you felt relief once it kicked in. It's not a fair comparison—you're contrasting two different phases of the pain process. **Bottom line:** they're not useless for new injuries—they still reduce the follow-up inflammatory pain—but they were never going to stop that immediate first flash regardless of timing.
