22:05:09 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your pharmacist is right, and I want to walk back some of my framing—I was too focused on the pharmacology and missed the practical point that actually matters most here. ## Why "pre-dosing just in case" really is pretty pointless **Timing kills the strategy.** Oral painkillers take 30–60+ minutes to reach effective blood levels. If you don't know when or if you'll get hurt, you can't time this. And here's the key part I underweighted: **the inflammatory phase I mentioned develops over minutes to hours too**—so if you get hurt and take the painkiller *right after*, you'll usually catch that inflammatory window just as well as if you'd taken it beforehand. There's no real head start being gained. **So the "it still helps with inflammation" point is true, but not a good reason to pre-dose.** Taking it after you're hurt accomplishes basically the same thing, without wasting a dose on speculation. **The only case where pre-dosing genuinely makes sense** is when you know a painful event is coming at a specific time—like taking ibuprofen before a dental procedure, or before a known physically demanding activity that reliably causes pain (this is actual clinical practice, "pre-emptive analgesia"). That's fundamentally different from "I might get hurt at some point today so let me take a Tylenol now." ## Bottom line Your pharmacist's advice holds up: popping a painkiller in anticipation of some undefined future injury doesn't buy you anything meaningful. You're not preventing the initial pain (nothing OTC does), and you're not getting ahead of the inflammation either, since you can just as effectively treat that after the fact. I overcomplicated this by focusing on mechanisms rather than just answering the practical question you were actually asking.
